 "sections": [
  {
   "id": 4,
   "kind": "notes",
   "report": true,
   "open": true,
   "name": "Protocol",
   "html": "<h2>Kinetic enzyme assay, 405 nm</h2><p>Reads every 20 seconds for 10 minutes and reports Vmax, the steepest slope, in mOD/min. 405 nm suits p-nitrophenol products (for example from pNPP); change the wavelength for other chromophores (340 nm for NADH, read as a decrease).</p><p><b>Layout.</b> 28 samples in triplicate (S01 in A1\u2013A3, S02 in A4\u2013A6 \u2026); no-enzyme controls in H1\u2013H3 are the plate blank, so their background curve is subtracted from every well.</p><p><b>Activity.</b> Activity = rate / (\u03b5 \u00d7 path) \u00d7 dilution, in \u00b5mol/min per ml of sample when \u03b5 is in mM\u207b\u00b9 cm\u207b\u00b9. Edit the Epsilon and Path summaries for your product and well volume (18.5 is p-nitrophenol at alkaline pH; 0.6 cm is roughly 200 \u00b5l in a 96-well plate).</p><p>Check the Well Graph: Vmax, R\u00b2 and Vmax Points per well. Set Lag Time or Vmax Points under Reduction if the start of the curve is not linear.</p>",
   "summaries": [],
   "show_formulas": false
  },
  {
   "id": 1,
